A few years back, I used to play a multi-user dungeon game called
Fantasia.  There was a single (extremely large) dungeon, with places
like stores or castles at known locations.  Everyone played in the same
dungeon at the same time.

When players were separated by distance, they were not synchronised in
real-time or turns, so there was no particular problem with someone pausing
to ponder.

When players were close, they played in real-time, so a faster typist
had an edge.  People could kill each other, in order to inherit their
experience and possesions.

There was no particular single goal, unlike nethack.  One thing you
might wish to do was to become the king of the dungeon -- you could
periodically levy a tax on the inhabitants, which they can't refuse to
pay.  This was a good way to get rich, if there were other players in
the dungeon.

An alternative thing would be to reduce your sin level (by good clean
dungeon living) to the point where your god would show you glimpes of
the holy grail -- if you actually managed to obtain the grail, you became
a god yourself, until such time as another character managed to obtain the
grail.  As a god, you could e.g. create vicious monsters in the vicinity
of humans you didn't like....

There was a bug in the multi-user code, however, involving a race condition
in the death-notification routine.  If you were fast, you could kill another
character, move away, move back, and if you beat the race condition, find
the character there to be killed again -- doubling the gain you'd
have gotten fairly.  This was a good way to create a high-level character,
if you had two terminals at your disposal.

Anyway, just some ideas along the multi-user line....
 
